.TH newgrp "" "Command"
.SH NAME
newgrp \- change to a new group
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBnewgrp \fIgroup\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
newgrp -- change to a new group
Usage:	newgrp group
.HE
.B newgrp
changes the caller's group identification to
.I group
and starts a new shell,
.BR /bin/sh ,
under that group id.
Access is checked against the entry for
.I group
in
.BR /etc/group :
membership is granted if the caller's login name appears in the
group's member list, if the group has no password and no member
list, or, failing that, if the caller supplies the group's password
correctly.
A member name prefixed with
.B !
in the member list is instead explicitly denied access.
.PP
On success,
.B newgrp
sets its real and effective group id to that of
.I group
and its real and effective user id back to the caller's own user id,
then execs
.B /bin/sh
as a login shell.
On failure it prints a short message and execs an ordinary
.B /bin/sh
without changing group.
.SH FILES
.B /etc/group
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR login ,
.B sh
.SH NOTES
.B newgrp
is normally installed set-user-id to root so that it can read the
encrypted password in
.BR /etc/group ;
it always drops back to the caller's own user id before running the
shell.
